--TEST--
A refused `Core\Sse->receive` is a throw the program catches and nothing more: the stream it was called on is still open and its next event is on the wire — `rule:concurrency/two-doors-one-isolate`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The refusal is a `LogicError` and not a fatal, so a helper written against
// `Core\Sse` can try the wait, catch the door it is standing in, and carry on
// writing -- which is the whole reason both doors share one type. A refusal
// that tore the stream down would make the shared helper a lie.
var $events = Core\Out::capture(fn (): void => {
    var $sse = Core\Sse::stream();
    $sse->send("ready");
    try {
        $sse->receive();
    } catch (LogicError $refused) {
        $sse->send("nothing to wait for", event: "note");
    }
    $sse->send("done");
});
echo $events;
--EXPECT--
data: ready

event: note
data: nothing to wait for

data: done

